4. Performance Guidelines
4.1 Hard Performance Targets
Metric	Target	Measurement Tool
Cold Start	< 2 seconds (warm < 1s)	Firebase Performance / Xcode Instruments
Frame Rate	60fps minimum, 0 jank frames during scroll	Android Studio Profiler / Xcode GPU Debug
API Response Time	< 500ms (p95) for newsletter fetch	Micrometer + Prometheus
App Size	< 50MB initial download	Build analysis
Memory Usage	< 200MB peak on mid-range devices	Profiler tools
Crash-Free Rate	> 99.8%	Firebase Crashlytics
ANR Rate (Android)	< 0.1%	Play Console Vitals
Battery Drain	< 3% per 10 minutes active use	Battery profiling
Offline Availability	Last newsletter available fully offline	SQLDelight local cache

4.2 Performance Implementation Rules
•	Lazy loading everywhere — Use LazyColumn / LazyRow for all lists. Never load all newsletter items at once.
•	Image optimization — Use Coil (KMP-compatible) with disk caching, progressive loading, and WebP format. Limit infographic images to max 1080px width.
•	Pagination — Archive endpoint must return paginated results (20 items/page). Implement infinite scroll with prefetch.
•	Background work — All API calls and DB operations on Dispatchers.IO. Never block the main/UI thread.
•	Startup optimization — Defer non-critical SDK init (analytics, crash reporting) until after first frame renders. Use async init where possible.
•	Compose optimization — Mark stable classes with @Stable / @Immutable. Use remember and derivedStateOf to prevent unnecessary recompositions. Avoid allocations in @Composable functions.
•	Network efficiency — Implement HTTP caching headers (ETag, Cache-Control). Use Ktor's content negotiation for GZIP compression. Retry with exponential backoff (max 3 retries).
•	Database indexing — Index user_id, created_date, and tech_stack_tag columns in PostgreSQL. Use EXPLAIN ANALYZE on all queries during development.
 
5. Design Guidelines
5.1 Design System Foundation
Element	Specification
Design Language	Material Design 3 (Material You) with custom brand tokens
Color Scheme	Dynamic color from device wallpaper (Android), curated palette (iOS). Dark mode mandatory.
Typography	Inter (body) + JetBrains Mono (code snippets). Scale: Display → Headline → Title → Body → Label
Spacing Grid	4dp base unit. Content padding: 16dp. Card gaps: 12dp.
Corner Radius	Cards: 16dp. Buttons: 12dp. Chips/tags: 8dp.
Elevation	Cards: 2dp rest, 8dp pressed. Bottom nav: 4dp.
Animations	Use spring() physics for natural feel. Duration: 200-400ms for transitions. No animation > 500ms.

5.2 Infographic Card Design Rules
•	Each card MUST have a visual hierarchy: Icon/emoji → Title → Key stat/number → Supporting text → Source attribution
•	Use data visualization where possible: progress bars, mini bar charts, trend arrows (↑↓→), percentage donuts
•	Color-code by section: Red = What's Hot, Blue = New Releases, Green = Quick Tips, Yellow = Learning Path, Purple = Deep Dive
•	Cards must be shareable as standalone images — render with proper padding and branding when exported
•	Maximum 3 font sizes per card. Maximum 4 colors per card (including background).
•	Infographic style reference: Think Morning Brew newsletter cards, Superhuman AI newsletter layout

5.3 UX Patterns
Pattern	Implementation
Skeleton Screens	Show content placeholders (shimmer effect) while loading — never a blank screen or spinner
Pull-to-Refresh	On newsletter and learning plan screens
Bottom Navigation	4 tabs: Home (Newsletter), Learn (Plan), Archive, Profile
Haptic Feedback	On bookmark tap, streak milestone, section swipe completion
Empty States	Illustrated empty states with actionable CTA (e.g., "Select your tech stack to get started")
Error States	Friendly error messages with retry button. Never show raw error codes or stack traces.
Accessibility	Minimum touch target 48x48dp. Content descriptions on all icons. Support TalkBack/VoiceOver. Dynamic type scaling.

5.4 Design Reference Sources
Use Mobbin (https://mobbin.com/) for UI pattern research across hundreds of real iOS & Android apps. Study the apps listed in Section 6 for specific UI/UX patterns.
 
