7. Do's and Don'ts
7.1 Architecture & Code
✅ DO	❌ DON'T
Follow Clean Architecture (domain → data → presentation) with clear dependency rules	Don't put business logic in Composables or ViewModels directly
Use expect/actual declarations for platform-specific code (e.g., file system, biometrics)	Don't use #if ANDROID style conditional compilation — use proper KMP abstractions
Write shared code in commonMain first; move to platform-specific only when necessary	Don't duplicate logic across androidMain and iosMain
Use dependency injection (Koin — KMP-compatible)	Don't create singletons or global state manually
Use Kotlin Coroutines + Flow for all async operations	Don't use callbacks or raw threads
Use SQLDelight for local storage (type-safe, cross-platform)	Don't use platform-specific ORMs (Room, Core Data)
Version all API endpoints (/api/v1/, /api/v2/)	Don't break existing API contracts without versioning
Use sealed classes for UI state (Loading, Success, Error)	Don't use boolean flags for state management
Use Ktor for HTTP client (KMP-native)	Don't use Retrofit (Android-only) or Alamofire (iOS-only)

7.2 AI/LLM Integration
✅ DO	❌ DON'T
Use structured prompts with clear output format (JSON schema)	Don't send open-ended prompts and hope for consistent output
Implement prompt versioning — store prompt templates in config/DB	Don't hardcode prompts in source code
Set temperature=0.3 for newsletter generation (factual, consistent)	Don't use high temperature (>0.7) for content that needs to be reliable
Validate LLM output against expected JSON schema before storing	Don't trust LLM output blindly — it can hallucinate URLs and facts
Implement fallback: if LLM API fails, serve cached content from last week	Don't let the entire app break because an AI API is down
Log token usage and cost per newsletter generation	Don't ignore API costs — they scale with users
Rate-limit LLM calls — batch user requests, generate once per cohort	Don't call LLM separately for each user if they share the same tech stack

7.3 Performance
✅ DO	❌ DON'T
Profile on mid-range devices (Pixel 6a, iPhone SE) — not flagships	Don't only test on Pixel 9 Pro / iPhone 16 Pro Max
Implement offline-first: cache last newsletter in SQLDelight	Don't require network for basic app functionality
Use LazyColumn with key parameter for stable list rendering	Don't use Column with forEach for dynamic lists
Compress images server-side before sending to mobile (WebP, <200KB)	Don't send full-resolution images to mobile clients
Implement proper lifecycle handling — cancel coroutines on screen exit	Don't leak coroutines or network calls when user navigates away
Pre-generate newsletter PDFs server-side during cron job	Don't generate PDFs on-device — it's CPU/memory intensive

7.4 Security
✅ DO	❌ DON'T
Use HTTPS everywhere. Pin certificates for API calls.	Don't allow HTTP or skip SSL verification even in debug
Store tokens in platform keystore (Android Keystore / iOS Keychain)	Don't store JWT tokens in SharedPreferences / UserDefaults
Implement token refresh with sliding expiry	Don't use long-lived tokens (>24h)
Sanitize all user input before sending to LLM prompts (prevent prompt injection)	Don't pass raw user input into LLM prompts
Use parameterized queries for all PostgreSQL operations	Don't concatenate SQL strings
Implement rate limiting on all API endpoints (100 req/min/user)	Don't leave APIs open to abuse
Obfuscate release builds (ProGuard/R8 for Android)	Don't ship debug builds to production

7.5 UX & Design
✅ DO	❌ DON'T
Support dynamic text scaling (accessibility)	Don't use fixed pixel sizes for text
Provide haptic feedback on meaningful interactions	Don't overuse haptics — only on bookmarks, streaks, completions
Show skeleton/shimmer screens while loading	Don't show blank screens or generic spinners
Design for thumb-zone reachability (bottom navigation, FABs)	Don't put primary actions at the top of the screen
Test on both LTR and RTL layouts from day one	Don't assume left-to-right only
Support landscape mode on tablets gracefully	Don't crash or clip content in landscape orientation
Make the newsletter feel like a curated "magazine" — not a feed	Don't create another RSS reader with plain text lists

7.6 Testing
✅ DO	❌ DON'T
Write unit tests for all use cases and repository implementations	Don't skip tests for "simple" logic
Use Turbine for testing Kotlin Flows	Don't use Thread.sleep() in tests
Write UI tests for critical flows (onboarding, newsletter load, bookmark)	Don't rely only on manual testing
Test on real iOS devices — simulator misses real-world issues	Don't ship iOS without real device testing
Implement screenshot testing for infographic cards (Paparazzi / Roborazzi)	Don't manually verify visual layouts across releases
Aim for 80%+ code coverage in commonMain	Don't chase 100% coverage — focus on business logic

7.7 Deployment & DevOps
✅ DO	❌ DON'T
Automate builds via GitHub Actions (separate workflows for Android/iOS/Backend)	Don't rely on manual builds and uploads
Use feature flags (Firebase Remote Config) for gradual rollouts	Don't deploy new features to 100% of users on day one
Implement app update prompts for breaking API changes	Don't let old app versions call deprecated APIs silently
Set up Firebase Crashlytics + Performance Monitoring from day one	Don't wait for production issues to add observability
Use semantic versioning (1.2.3) and maintain a CHANGELOG	Don't use arbitrary version numbers
 
8. Non-Functional Requirements
Category	Requirement
Availability	99.9% uptime for backend API
Scalability	Support 10K → 100K users without architecture changes
Data Retention	Keep newsletter archive for 1 year. Allow user data export (GDPR).
Localization	English v1. Architect all strings via resource files for future i18n.
Accessibility	WCAG 2.1 AA compliance. TalkBack + VoiceOver support.
Min OS Versions	Android 8.0 (API 26) / iOS 16.0
App Store Compliance	Follow Apple HIG and Google Material guidelines. Privacy nutrition labels required for iOS.
 
9. Suggested Implementation Phases
Phase	Scope	Duration
Phase 1 — Foundation	Project setup, KMP structure, design system, auth, tech stack selector, basic navigation	2-3 weeks
Phase 2 — Backend Core	Spring Boot API, PostgreSQL schema, content aggregation cron, LLM integration, Redis caching	2-3 weeks
Phase 3 — Newsletter MVP	Newsletter fetch + render, infographic cards, section navigation, offline cache	2-3 weeks
Phase 4 — Learning Plan	Learning path generation, progress tracking, streak counter, stats dashboard	2 weeks
Phase 5 — Polish	Share/export (PDF/image), push notifications (FCM), dark mode, animations, accessibility	2 weeks
Phase 6 — Quality	Performance profiling, security audit, UI/screenshot tests, beta testing (TestFlight + Firebase)	1-2 weeks
Phase 7 — Launch	App Store + Play Store submission, monitoring dashboards, post-launch fixes	1 week
 
10. Critical Success Metrics (Post-Launch KPIs)
Metric	Target
Weekly Active Users (WAU)	Track growth week-over-week
Newsletter Open Rate	> 60% of registered users open weekly newsletter
Learning Plan Completion	> 30% of users complete at least 3 items/week
Average Streak Length	> 4 consecutive weeks
App Store Rating	> 4.5 stars
Crash-Free Sessions	> 99.8%
Cold Start Time	< 2 seconds (p95)


— End of Document —

This plan is designed to be agent-ready — any coding agent (Cursor, Copilot Workspace, Devin, or a human dev team) can pick up a phase and start building with clear boundaries, acceptance criteria, and guardrails.
